`grep -n "check_extra" src/test.cpp` returns only the signature line and that comment. So `CheckAllBits` vs `CheckIdsBit` vs `0` produce **identical behavior**.

## Algorithm, in order

**Step A — quantized vertex position index** (`Quantized_Vertices`, test.cpp:402-463). `scale = 2.0f / epsilon = 2000.0f`; a position hashes to `(floor(2000*x), floor(2000*y), floor(2000*z))` stored in a `std::map<tuple<int32,int32,int32>, uint32_t>` mapping cell -> vertex id. `find(v)` scans the **3x3x3 neighborhood of cells** and returns the first vertex id found (no actual distance check — so matching tolerance is ~0.0005 guaranteed, up to ~0.0015 possible along an axis).

```cpp
std::optional<uint32_t> find(Vec3 v) {
	auto [x, y, z] = quantize(v);
	for (int32_t dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++)
	 for (int32_t dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++)
	  for (int32_t dz = -1; dz <= 1; dz++)
	   if (vertices.find({x + dx, y + dy, z + dz}) != vertices.end())
	    return vertices.at({x + dx, y + dy, z + dz});
	return std::nullopt;
}
```

**Step B — uniqueness precondition.** Both meshes are indexed, then:
```cpp
if (!source_verts.all_unique()) return "Source mesh does not have epsilon-unique vertex positions.";
if (!target_verts.all_unique()) return "Target mesh does not have epsilon-unique vertex positions.";
```
`all_unique()` fails if two vertices of the *same* mesh land in the same or an adjacent cell. **Any mesh with (near-)coincident vertex positions cannot be compared at all** — it fails with this message regardless of correctness.

**Step C — bidirectional vertex matching by POSITION ONLY** (ids are just lookup keys, never compared):
```cpp
for (VertexCRef v = source.vertices.begin(); v != source.vertices.end(); v++) {
	if (auto target_id = target_verts.find(v->position)) {
		v_target_to_source.insert({*target_id, v});
	} else {
		return "Source vertex set is not a subset of target vertex set!";
	}
}
// symmetric loop -> v_source_to_target, else "Target vertex set is not a subset of source vertex set!"
```

**Step D — edges matched as unordered vertex pairs, injectively** (multiplicity-aware; walks the *other* mesh's one-ring):
```cpp
for (EdgeCRef e = source.edges.begin(); e != source.edges.end(); e++) {
	uint32_t id0 = e->halfedge->vertex->id;
	uint32_t id1 = e->halfedge->twin->vertex->id;
	VertexCRef v0 = v_source_to_target.at(id0);
	VertexCRef v1 = v_source_to_target.at(id1);
	bool found = false;
	HalfedgeCRef h = v0->halfedge;
	do {
		if (h->twin->vertex == v1) {
			auto match = h->edge;
			if (e_target_ids.find(match->id) == e_target_ids.end()) {
				found = true;
				e_target_ids.insert(match->id);
				break;
			}
		}
		h = h->twin->next;
	} while (h != v0->halfedge);
	if (!found) return "Source edge set is not a subset of target edge set!";
}
```
plus the symmetric target→source loop ("Target edge set is not a subset of source edge set!"). Consumed target-edge ids are recorded in an `unordered_set` so parallel/duplicate edges must match 1:1 in count.

**Step E — faces matched as an unordered MULTISET of vertices + boundary flag, injectively:**
```cpp
std::unordered_multiset<VertexCRef> verts;
{ HalfedgeCRef h = f->halfedge;
  do { verts.insert(v_source_to_target.at(h->vertex->id)); h = h->next; } while (h != f->halfedge); }

bool found = false;
HalfedgeCRef begin = (*verts.begin())->halfedge;
HalfedgeCRef h = begin;
do {
	FaceCRef match = h->face;
	std::unordered_multiset<VertexCRef> remaining_verts = verts;
	HalfedgeCRef h_match = match->halfedge;
	do {
		auto v = remaining_verts.find(h_match->vertex);
		if (v != remaining_verts.end()) remaining_verts.erase(v);
		h_match = h_match->next;
	} while (h_match != match->halfedge);

	if (remaining_verts.empty() && f_target_ids.find(match->id) == f_target_ids.end() &&
	    match->boundary == f->boundary) {
		found = true;
		f_target_ids.insert(match->id);
		break;
	}
	h = h->twin->next;
} while (h != begin);
if (!found) return "Source face set is not a subset of target face set!";
```
plus the symmetric loop ("Target face set is not a subset of source face set!"). Candidate faces are only those incident to `(*verts.begin())` (an arbitrary vertex of the face, order from an `unordered_multiset`). Then:
```cpp
assert(f_source_ids.size() == source.faces.size());
assert(f_target_ids.size() == target.faces.size());
assert(f_source_ids.size() == f_target_ids.size());
```
(asserts are live — the Linux build uses `-O2 -g` with no `-DNDEBUG`, Maekfile.js:57-62, 365).

## Direct answers
- **By vertex position matching** — yes, that is the sole identity criterion, via 3x3x3-cell quantized lookup at eps = 0.001 (scale 2000).
- **By element ids** — no. IDs are used only as hash-map keys and as "already consumed" tokens for injectivity. No id value is ever required to be equal between the two meshes.
- **By ordering of element lists** — no. `vertices`/`edges`/`faces`/`halfedges` list order is irrelevant; every comparison is a set/multiset match. Iteration order only affects which arbitrary candidate is greedily consumed first.
- **Canonicalization** — no explicit canonical form; the "canonicalization" is implicit: positions are quantized to a 0.0005-unit grid, and each element is reduced to (unordered vertex pair) for edges / (unordered vertex multiset + boundary bit) for faces.
- **Not checked at all**: halfedge count, `next`/`twin` structure, **face winding / cyclic vertex order** (a face `{0,1,2,3}` matches `{0,2,1,3}` and matches reversed winding), edge `sharp`, corner data, bone weights, ids, list order. Also the face test is a *subset-erase* test, so a candidate face with **extra** vertices can still match if it contains all of the query face's vertices (the reverse-direction pass + injectivity limit but do not fully eliminate this).
- Two-way subset + injective consumption does imply equal element counts for vertices/edges/faces.
- Mesh structural validity is *not* checked here; tests call `mesh.validate()` separately (see `/workspace/tests/a2/test.a2.l1.cpp:6-8`).
- Crash surface: `(*verts.begin())->halfedge` and `v0->halfedge` assume every vertex has valid connectivity — a malformed mesh will UB rather than return an error string.

# 2. Flag bits (`/workspace/src/test.h:74-84`)

```cpp
using CheckExtra = uint8_t;
enum : CheckExtra {
	CheckIdsBit = 0x01,
	CheckBoneWeightsBit = 0x02,
	CheckCornerNormalsBit = 0x04,
	CheckCornerUVsBit = 0x08,
	CheckSharpBit = 0x10,
	CheckAllBits = 0xff
};
//checks position + whatever you pass in check_extra:
static std::optional<std::string> differs(Halfedge_Mesh const &a, Halfedge_Mesh const &b, CheckExtra check_extra = CheckIdsBit);
```
Intended meanings: `0x01` element ids, `0x02` `Vertex::bone_weights`, `0x04` `Halfedge::corner_normal`, `0x08` `Halfedge::corner_uv`, `0x10` `Edge::sharp`; `CheckAllBits = 0xff` = all of them. **All are dead in the current implementation.** Default when omitted is `CheckIdsBit` — also a no-op. Nothing else in `src/` or `tests/` references these constants except test.h and the 8 a2 call sites.

# 3. corner_uv / corner_normal / bone_weights / sharp

**Not compared. No tolerance applies — the code paths do not exist.** `Test::differs(Halfedge_Mesh,...)` reads only `position`, `id`, `boundary`, and connectivity pointers. Confirmed by grep: `corner_uv`, `corner_normal`, `bone_weights`, `sharp` appear nowhere in `/workspace/src/test.cpp`. The only test-suite touchpoint is `/workspace/tests/a4/test.a4.task3.skin.cpp` (uses `skeleton.assign_bone_weights(&mesh)` and compares skinned `pos`/`norm` with the scalar `Test::differs`, not the mesh overload). No a2 test compares uvs/normals.

Scalar tolerance used everywhere else is `Test::differs_eps = 0.001f` (test.h:57), absolute:
```cpp
bool Test::differs(float a, float b) {
	if (std::isnan(a) && std::isnan(b)) return false;
	if (std::isnan(a) || std::isnan(b)) return true;
	return std::abs(a-b) > differs_eps;
}
```
Vec2/Vec3/Vec4/Mat4/Spectrum/vector overloads are componentwise on that. `PT::Trace::distance` is the exception, using `EPS_F`. Note the mesh overload uses its own local `constexpr float epsilon = 0.001f`, not `differs_eps`, and it is a grid-quantization tolerance, not an absolute-distance one.

# 4. Registration and running

**Registration**: a global `Test` object per test case; the constructor inserts into a function-local `static std::map<std::string, std::function<void()>>`, name lowercased, duplicate names call `die()`:
```cpp
Test::Test(const std::string& name, const std::function<void()>& test) {
	auto ret = get_tests().emplace(to_lower(name), test);
	if (!ret.second) die("Two tests named '%s'.", name.c_str());
}
```
Usage form: `Test test_a2_l1_flip_edge_basic_simple("a2.l1.flip_edge.basic.simple", []() { ... });`. Failure = throw `Test::error`; `Test::ignored` = skipped. `Maekfile.js:228-241` compiles **every** `.cpp` under `tests/` recursively and links them into the single `Scotty3D` binary (`Maekfile.js:24, 243-288`), so no separate test binary exists.

**Selection is a case-insensitive SUBSTRING match, not a prefix** (despite the CLI help text):
```cpp
std::string prefix = to_lower(prefix_);
...
if (test.first.find(prefix) != std::string::npos) { ... }
```

**Command lines** (binary at `/workspace/Scotty3D`, already built; rebuild with `node Maekfile.js` from `/workspace`):
- All tests: `/workspace/Scotty3D --run-tests` (the option is `expected(0,1)`, so the value may be omitted; empty string matches everything)
- A group: `/workspace/Scotty3D --run-tests a2.` or `... --run-tests a2.l1`
- A single test: `/workspace/Scotty3D --run-tests a2.l1.flip_edge.basic.simple`
- Exit code 0 if zero failures, 1 otherwise (`src/main.cpp:77-83`). Ignored tests do not fail the run.

